---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import TryItNow from '../../components/TryItNow.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/CopyPrompt.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Connectors & Skills"
  description="What Claude is wired into at Aptera — and the repeatable skills you can run by name."
  pageId="connectors"
  readMinutes={8}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="🔌"
    title="Connectors & Skills"
    subtitle="Claude isn't just a chatbot. At Aptera it's wired into your actual tools — and it knows some tricks."
    readMinutes={8}
  />

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      A <strong>connector</strong> is a secure bridge that lets Claude read and write a tool on your behalf.
      A <strong>skill</strong> is a packaged, repeatable workflow you can run by name. Together they turn
      Claude from a clever assistant into something that actually does work inside your systems.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      Out of the box, Claude knows a lot — but it doesn't know what's in your Jira board, your
      Confluence wiki, or last month's Ramp statement. Connectors fix that. They give Claude
      permission to look things up, create records, and take actions in the tools you already use.
      You stay in control: Claude acts as you, with your permissions, and asks before it does
      anything consequential.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      Under the hood, connectors are built on the <strong>Model Context Protocol (MCP)</strong> — an
      open standard for plugging AI into outside tools and data. You don't need to think about it day to
      day; it's simply the common "plug" that lets Claude reach the systems you already use.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="my-6 rounded-r-[12px] border-l-[3px] border-gold bg-gold/5 px-5 py-4">
    <blockquote class="m-0 text-lg italic leading-relaxed text-text">
      "Think of MCP like a USB-C port for AI applications. Just as USB-C provides a standardized way to
      connect electronic devices, MCP provides a standardized way to connect AI applications to external
      systems."
    </blockquote>
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gold">— Anthropic, Model Context Protocol documentation</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!-- Connected Today -->
  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Connected today</h2>
    <p class="mb-5 text-muted">These are the tools Claude is already wired into at Aptera. Open Co-Work and start asking.</p>

    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">

      <!-- Jira -->
      <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
        <div class="mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-2xl">🎫</span>
          <span class="font-bold text-text text-lg">Jira</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Read, create, and update issues. Claude can search across projects, pull sprint status, or
          log a new bug without you touching the Jira UI.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-dim mb-1">Try asking:</p>
        <p class="text-sm italic text-muted">"What are the open issues in project OI assigned to me right now?"</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Confluence -->
      <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
        <div class="mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-2xl">📚</span>
          <span class="font-bold text-text text-lg">Confluence</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Read, create, and update pages. Ask Claude to find documentation, summarize a space, or
          draft a new page and publish it — straight from a conversation.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-dim mb-1">Try asking:</p>
        <p class="text-sm italic text-muted">"Find the onboarding docs in Confluence and give me a bullet summary of what's there."</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Ramp -->
      <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
        <div class="mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-2xl">💳</span>
          <span class="font-bold text-text text-lg">Ramp</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Transactions, bills, reimbursements, and spend reporting. Ask Claude to pull your recent
          charges, flag anything that needs a receipt, or summarize team spend by category.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-dim mb-1">Try asking:</p>
        <p class="text-sm italic text-muted">"Show me my transactions from the last 30 days and flag any that are missing receipts."</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Microsoft 365 -->
      <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
        <div class="mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-2xl">📨</span>
          <span class="font-bold text-text text-lg">Microsoft 365</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Outlook mail and calendar, SharePoint file search, and Teams chat history. Claude can
          check your schedule, hunt down a file, or search chat for that decision from last month.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-dim mb-1">Try asking:</p>
        <p class="text-sm italic text-muted">"Search my Teams messages for any mention of the solar hood pilot from the last two weeks."</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="chat" title="🔌 How do I turn one on?">
    <p>
      Connectors live in the <strong>Claude desktop app</strong> (Co-Work) — see
      <a class="text-chat underline" href={href('/apps/')}>Apps Compared</a> and
      <a class="text-chat underline" href={href('/getting-started/')}>Getting Started</a>. The ones above
      are already set up for Aptera, so you can open Co-Work and start asking. The first time Claude uses a
      connector it may ask you to sign in and authorize it; after that, it acts as you.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="⚠️ Connectors act as you">
    <p>
      When Claude uses a connector, it's acting with <strong>your identity and your permissions</strong>.
      If you ask Claude to create a Jira issue, it creates it as you. If you ask it to send an email,
      it sends it from your address. Claude will always confirm before taking actions that write or
      modify data — but you're the one responsible for what gets done. Treat it like handing your
      keyboard to a very capable colleague: you're still accountable. A connector can only reach what
      <strong>you</strong> already have access to, and the same rules from
      <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/safety/')}>Safety &amp; Data</a> apply.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Skills -->
  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="mb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Skills</h2>
    <p class="mb-5 text-muted leading-relaxed">
      A skill is a packaged, repeatable workflow you can invoke by name — like calling a function
      instead of writing it from scratch every time. Skills combine multiple steps (including connector
      calls) into a single, reliable flow — <strong>and you can build your own</strong> (more on that
      below). The flagship one available right now:
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-cowork/40 bg-cowork-dim/20 p-6">
      <div class="mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="text-2xl">📝</span>
        <span class="text-lg font-bold text-text">Meeting Minutes skill</span>
        <span class="ml-auto rounded-full border border-cowork/40 bg-card px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-cowork">Co-Work</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Drop a Teams meeting transcript (VTT or TXT file) into Co-Work, then run the Meeting Minutes
        skill. It takes care of the rest — formatted Confluence notes and Jira action items, all linked
        together. Here's exactly how it goes:
      </p>

      <ol class="space-y-3 text-text text-sm leading-relaxed list-none p-0 m-0">
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-cowork/20 text-cowork font-bold text-xs">1</span>
          <span><strong>Upload the transcript.</strong> Drag your .vtt or .txt Teams export into the Co-Work file area. Claude reads the whole thing.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-cowork/20 text-cowork font-bold text-xs">2</span>
          <span><strong>Claude confirms the details.</strong> It identifies attendees and the meeting title, then asks you to verify before writing anything. You approve, correct, or add context.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-cowork/20 text-cowork font-bold text-xs">3</span>
          <span><strong>Confluence page is created.</strong> A formatted meeting minutes page goes into the correct Confluence space — attendees, summary, discussion points, decisions, and action items.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-cowork/20 text-cowork font-bold text-xs">4</span>
          <span><strong>Jira issues are filed.</strong> Each action item becomes a Jira ticket, assigned to the right person, linked back to the Confluence page. No copy-paste, no missed items.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-cowork/20 text-cowork font-bold text-xs">5</span>
          <span><strong>You review and share.</strong> Claude gives you links to everything it created. You spot-check, tweak anything that needs it, and send the Confluence link to the team.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
        A 90-minute meeting that used to take 45 minutes to document now takes about 3.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Build your own skill -->
  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="mb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Build your own skill</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
      Here's the part most people miss: you can <strong>make your own skills</strong>, and you don't need
      to know how to code. If you keep doing the same multi-step task the same way, that's a skill waiting
      to happen — a weekly report Finance rebuilds by hand, a QA checklist Manufacturing fills out the same
      way each time, a change-order summary in CAD, issue triage in Engineering. (It's the close cousin of
      an <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/agentic-looping/')}>agentic loop</a>.)
    </p>

    <figure class="my-6 rounded-r-[12px] border-l-[3px] border-gold bg-gold/5 px-5 py-4">
      <blockquote class="m-0 text-lg italic leading-relaxed text-text">
        "Anyone can now specialize their agents with composable capabilities by capturing and sharing their
        procedural knowledge."
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gold">— Anthropic, on Agent Skills</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="mb-2 text-muted leading-relaxed">The way to build one is to describe it to Claude:</p>
    <ol class="mb-5 space-y-2 text-text text-sm leading-relaxed list-decimal pl-5">
      <li><strong>Talk it through.</strong> Tell Claude the repetitive task — the steps, the inputs, what "done" looks like — and ask whether it's a good skill candidate.</li>
      <li><strong>Claude drafts the skill.</strong> It packages the instructions (and any connector steps) into a skill you can run by name.</li>
      <li><strong>Test it</strong> on a real example and refine until it's reliable.</li>
      <li><strong>Bring it to IT to publish.</strong> We'll help make it available to you — and share it with your team if it's broadly useful.</li>
    </ol>

    <TryItNow title="Start a skill with Claude">
      <p>Describe a task you repeat. Paste this to kick it off — fill in the brackets:</p>
      <CopyPrompt text="I do this repetitive task and want to turn it into a reusable skill: [describe the task — the trigger, the steps you take, the tools involved, and what the finished result looks like]. First, tell me whether this is a good candidate for a skill. Then help me build it, and flag anything you'd need access to (like a connector)." />
    </TryItNow>

    <Callout variant="cowork" title="⌨️ For Claude Code users">
      <p>
        Claude Code includes a built-in <strong>skill-creator</strong> — ask it to make a skill and it
        scaffolds the files for you, version-controlled alongside your project.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout variant="gold" title="💡 Not sure if it's a good fit?">
      <p>
        Ask Claude first — it'll help you judge whether a task is worth turning into a skill. Then reach
        out to IT (<a class="text-gold underline" href="mailto:jake@aptera.us">jake@aptera.us</a>) when
        you're ready to publish it.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <!-- Want something connected? -->
  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="mb-2 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Want something connected?</h2>
    <p class="mb-4 text-muted leading-relaxed">
      The list above is just where we started. There are connectors available for dozens of tools —
      and if your team has a tool that's eating time, there's a good chance we can wire Claude into it.
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5 mb-4">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-dim mb-3">Tools with connectors available</p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {['NetSuite', 'Slack', 'Google Workspace', 'GitHub', 'Salesforce', 'HubSpot', 'Notion', 'Rippling', 'ServiceNow', 'Zendesk'].map((tool) => (
          <span class="rounded-full border border-border bg-card-hover px-3 py-1 text-sm text-muted">{tool}</span>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed">
      To request a new connector or skill, or to report an issue with an existing one, email
      <a href="mailto:jake@aptera.us" class="text-gold underline underline-offset-2 font-semibold">jake@aptera.us</a>.
      Include the tool name and a quick description of what you'd like Claude to be able to do with it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <TryItNow title="Try a connector">
    <p>
      Open Co-Work and use the Confluence connector to run a real search. Paste this prompt exactly:
    </p>
    <CopyPrompt text="Search Confluence for our onboarding docs and summarize what's there. Tell me: what topics are covered, what looks out of date, and what seems to be missing for a new employee." />
    <p>
      Claude will query Confluence on your behalf, pull back the relevant pages, and give you a
      structured summary — no Confluence tab required.
    </p>
  </TryItNow>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>Connectors give Claude <strong>live access to your tools</strong> — Jira, Confluence, Ramp, and Microsoft 365 are wired in today.</li>
      <li>Skills are <strong>named, repeatable workflows</strong> — the Meeting Minutes skill turns a Teams transcript into Confluence notes + Jira tickets in minutes.</li>
      <li><strong>You can build your own skill</strong> — describe a repetitive task to Claude, test it, and bring it to IT to publish.</li>
      <li>Connectors act as you and with your permissions — you're always the one approving consequential actions.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>
</BaseLayout>
